# The Dockerfile does `COPY . .`, so anything not excluded here is uploaded as
# build context and lands in the builder layer.
#
# Two things this is load-bearing for:
#
#   1. `target/` is tens of gigabytes after a few builds. Without this line a
#      remote build (Render, CI) spends its whole time budget uploading object
#      files it is about to overwrite, and may not finish at all.
#   2. `.env` holds real credentials. The final image is a separate stage that
#      copies only the binary and `scenarios/`, so nothing leaks into what ships
#      — but the *builder* layer would still contain it, and builder layers are
#      cached and often pushed.

# Build output — regenerated inside the image, never wanted from the host.
target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Credentials and local overrides.
.env
.env.*
!env.example

# Version control and editor state.
.git/
.gitignore
.vscode/
.qodo/

# Local infrastructure. The image runs against whatever the platform provides;
# compose is for the host.
compose.yaml
.devcontainer/
deploy/
.github/

# Logs and scratch files from local runs.
*.log
*.sqlite

# Documentation. HANDOFF.md is gitignored anyway; nothing in the build reads it.
HANDOFF.md
CHANGELOG.md
README.md

# NOT excluded, and must stay that way:
#   Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, crates/, server/  — the build itself
#   migrations/                               — `include_str!`d by crates/http
#   scenarios/                                — copied into the final image and
#                                               read at boot via TESTBED_SCENARIO
